#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111881 :tutorial/content "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) + 1 &= x \\\\\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= x - 1 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= \\frac{x - 1}{3} \\\\\n2^{\\log_{2} (y - 1)} &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny - 1 &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf6e7706-d6b7-5b75-ac3f-184a71b3330a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2818 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111905 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$. We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2^{5x} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1 \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2^{5y} \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= \\log_{2} (2^{5y}) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= 5y \\\\\n5y &= \\log_{2} (x - 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{5}\\log_{2} (x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc5609e8-50a8-59fe-af6c-4cfc16ccc454" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6516 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
